WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.659
You’re looking at a map of North Carolina

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:08.034
showing where the state’s over 2,000 
large-scale hog farms are located.

00:00:08.034 --> 00:00:12.457
They’re clustered primarily in this rural
region in the eastern part of the state.

00:00:12.457 --> 00:00:16.932
North Carolina is the 3rd biggest 
hog-producing state in the US.

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:20.632
But this level of concentration 
makes the state uniquely qualified

00:00:20.632 --> 00:00:24.820
to show how industrial hog farming
impacts the surrounding communities.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.140
Specifically when it comes to one thing: 
pig waste.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.617
That’s what this video is about, 
so for this story, we went there.

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:35.672
And when I spoke to locals in the area 

00:00:35.672 --> 00:00:39.889
they told me that the best way 
to understand this story is from the air.

00:00:47.707 --> 00:00:51.591
We flew in a small Cessna propellor plane
from New Bern, North Carolina

00:00:51.591 --> 00:00:54.539
to our first stop, over Duplin County,

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:57.252
which has the highest concentration 
of hog farms.

00:00:57.252 --> 00:00:59.870
BALDWIN: They have more hogs 
than they do people.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:05.350
Larry Baldwin works for the Waterkeeper Alliance,
a non-profit environmental watchdog group

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.035
and he regularly goes on flights like these
as a surveying tool.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:12.150
BALWIN: A lot of these places 
you cannot see from the ground

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.249
so you don't even know they're there.

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:20.400
So doing these flights, it's one of the more
important resources that we have.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.152
Hog facilities are easy to spot 
because of this tell-tale feature.

00:01:25.152 --> 00:01:29.292
The hog farm will always have 
what they call a lagoon.

00:01:29.292 --> 00:01:35.788
I refer to it as a cesspool 
and it's holding nothing but hog feces and urine.

00:01:35.788 --> 00:01:40.432
Historically, and on small farms today, 
pigs graze on pasture.

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:45.880
But in industrialized hog farming, 
farmers confine thousands of pigs in buildings.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.770
This makes pigs’ lives more brutal, 
without access to sun or fresh air

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.579
and it also creates a waste management problem.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.229
Enter: the lagoon system

00:01:56.229 --> 00:02:00.780
Inside the buildings, pigs drop their waste
onto slatted floors.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.777
And it gets flushed into the lagoons 
through pipes like these.

00:02:04.777 --> 00:02:07.936
Bacteria in the lagoons break down the waste.

00:02:07.936 --> 00:02:11.069
When they get too full, hog farmers 
syphon the waste

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:15.314
to a massive sprinkler and spray 
nearby crop fields with it.

00:02:15.314 --> 00:02:17.598
We saw this from the air.

00:02:17.598 --> 00:02:20.760
Manure is a good fertilizer, but 
most people told me

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.789
this is more of a waste disposal strategy
than a fertilizing one.

00:02:25.789 --> 00:02:28.747
Spraying and lagoons have air quality risks.

00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:32.680
When bacteria breaks the waste down, 
it releases hundreds of compounds

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:39.357
like methane, carbon dioxide, ammonia, 
hydrogen sulfide and dusts into the air.

00:02:39.357 --> 00:02:43.090
These can cause asthma, respiratory diseases,
headaches, and nausea

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:45.612
among other conditions.

00:02:45.612 --> 00:02:49.419
A recent study on US agriculture found that
“reduced air quality”

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:53.519
contributes to nearly 18,000 
premature deaths nationwide

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:58.418
mostly from ammonia emissions from
“livestock waste and fertilizer application.”

00:02:58.418 --> 00:03:00.613
That’s not even to mention the smell

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:04.146
which we picked up even from the air 
near the Duplin County Airport. 

00:03:14.748 --> 00:03:19.751
This lagoon and sprayfield system also impacts
surface and groundwaters.

00:03:19.751 --> 00:03:25.704
When waste gets into water it brings pollutants
like nitrates, phosphorous, and fecal bacteria.

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:29.036
Which can cause algae blooms, 
fish kills, and potentially

00:03:29.036 --> 00:03:31.902
life-threatening illness in humans.

00:03:31.902 --> 00:03:35.129
This can happen when lagoons flood 
after hurricanes

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:39.764
exacerbated by the fact that this region 
sits in a coastal floodplain.

00:03:39.764 --> 00:03:42.620
And some studies — including one of this
watershed

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.400
in eastern North Carolina —

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.720
show high levels of waste pollutants even
without rainfall or a breach

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.310
meaning lagoons pose a threat all the time.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.056
There’s evidence of pig waste 
underground, too.

00:03:55.056 --> 00:03:57.769
Which matters because people 
who live in this rural area

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:02.893
primarily use wells as a water source
and don’t have access to county water.

00:04:04.298 --> 00:04:08.540
All of these negative impacts disproportionately
affect some of the most vulnerable people

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.610
in the state.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:15.239
Residents here are more likely to be 
a person of color than the rest of the state.

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:18.631
And poverty rates are 
higher than average, too.

00:04:18.631 --> 00:04:22.693
Making this pollution more than 
an environmental issue.

00:04:22.693 --> 00:04:25.720
It’s an environmental justice issue.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:29.440
In 2018, North Carolina settled 
a federal civil rights complaint

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.040
filed by Larry Baldwin's organization 
and others

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.430
alleging racial discrimination by the state

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:40.750
due to the demographics of communities
where it issued hog facility permits.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:45.921
The complaint cited health issues, odors,
nuisances, increased expenses,

00:04:45.921 --> 00:04:49.800
social and psychological harm, 
and declining property values.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.272
BALDWIN: It's not the way to treat people
just so we can raise cheap meat.

00:04:56.272 --> 00:04:59.680
It's wrong on a number of different levels.

00:05:01.085 --> 00:05:05.450
During the flight, we flew over a community
surrounded by hog facilities:

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:09.295
the congregation of this church, 
in the town of Magnolia.

00:05:09.295 --> 00:05:12.300
In Google maps, I counted at least 
12 hog lagoons

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.375
within 2 and a half miles of the church.

00:05:15.375 --> 00:05:16.730
I'm the Reverend Jimmy Melvin.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.390
I’m the pastor here at Mount Zion 
AME Zion Church.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:25.520
I'm Danielle Koonce and I've been attending
my father's church here at Mount Zion.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.220
MELVIN: So many of our people, in a rural
setting, are used to farms.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.865
MELVIN: This is nature to them.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.450
MELVIN: This steeple used to be white.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:39.600
MELVING: We are now in the need of getting
our steeple pressure washed.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.397
Drone footage we shot shows why.

00:05:41.397 --> 00:05:44.651
Nearby hog farms would spray their fields
with manure

00:05:44.651 --> 00:05:47.043
and mist would reach the steeple.

00:05:47.043 --> 00:05:51.191
And recently this health department notice
was put on the church’s door.

00:05:51.191 --> 00:05:52.740
KOONCE: It was really clear warning.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:59.080
High nitrate levels don't use this water to
make infant formula for babies.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:04.810
The issue with hog farm waste is that 
it creates high nitrate levels

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:10.008
in the ground, in the streams, and 
the tributaries around that farm.

00:06:10.008 --> 00:06:13.570
It was just a no brainer that 
that is why there's

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.671
high nitrate levels in the groundwater.

00:06:16.671 --> 00:06:20.100
So, this is the new, deeper well?

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.270
Yeah, this is the new, deeper well right here.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.990
The church’s well was only 20 feet deep
at the time of the notice,

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:29.580
so the church paid nearly $4,000 
to dig the well deeper

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.968
to 250 feet, to bypass 
any groundwater contamination.

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:38.270
After a Sunday service in the fall of 2021

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:43.400
Reverend Melvin led a community meeting about
what parishioners were experiencing at home.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:48.693
Like if this is happening where a church
is getting notices for high nitrate levels

00:06:48.693 --> 00:06:52.440
then what is happening in 
the communities, in the houses,

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.160
in the neighborhoods that 
live around that church?

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:59.820
They have sat these hog farms 
in black communities.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.950
And then we can't have cookouts 
or family gatherings.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.447
Fighting flies and fighting the smell.

00:07:06.447 --> 00:07:08.391
As far as these hog houses are concerned.

00:07:08.391 --> 00:07:09.880
There's one behind my house

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:14.102
and when they spray the stuff 
it turns into like mist.

00:07:14.102 --> 00:07:15.844
Well you breathe this stuff.

00:07:15.844 --> 00:07:19.434
I grew up having bronchial asthma as a child.

00:07:19.434 --> 00:07:20.715
I went away.

00:07:20.715 --> 00:07:21.997
I was in a different environment.

00:07:21.997 --> 00:07:26.248
I moved back down here and I have 
two doctors, specialists

00:07:26.248 --> 00:07:31.258
I have to go to every two or three months
so I can keep my lungs cleared out.

00:07:31.258 --> 00:07:35.807
One of the specialists, he said a lot of people
that have the same thing that I have.

00:07:35.807 --> 00:07:39.786
It comes from being in an area where we have
the hogs and all that.

00:07:39.786 --> 00:07:43.380
Even our children graduate from high school
they go away to college.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.140
Some don't come back to the area.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.595
These children will not and 
I can understand that.

00:07:47.595 --> 00:07:51.055
I didn't encourage neither one of 
my daughters to live here.

00:07:51.055 --> 00:07:55.197
Clean water is not a black and white issue,
is humanity issue.

00:07:55.197 --> 00:07:56.997
Stories like the ones from Magnolia

00:07:56.997 --> 00:08:02.096
have been told since the spray field and
lagoon system started in the 1980s.

00:08:02.096 --> 00:08:05.460
Including a 1995 Pulitzer-Prize winning

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.691
Raleigh News &amp; Observer series 
on the hog industry.

00:08:08.691 --> 00:08:12.581
Which inspired one of North Carolina’s only
significant interventions.

00:08:12.581 --> 00:08:19.960
A moratorium in 1997 on new or expanded hog
farms, made permanent a few years later.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.500
And then this document almost transformed
the industry.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:26.960
It’s a 2000 agreement between North Carolina’s
Attorney General

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:29.085
and pork companies in the state.

00:08:29.085 --> 00:08:33.610
Including Smithfield Foods, the largest
hog producing company in North Carolina

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.490
and one of the largest in the world.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.750
In this agreement, Smithfield paid 
millions of dollars

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:44.532
to North Carolina State University to research
an “environmentally superior technology"

00:08:44.532 --> 00:08:48.987
so that the industry could do away with lagoons
and sprayfields for good.

00:08:48.987 --> 00:08:52.147
This new technology had to meet 3 criteria:

00:08:52.147 --> 00:08:54.000
be permittable by the state,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.137
substantially eliminate water and air pollution, 

00:08:57.137 --> 00:08:58.977
and be economically feasible.

00:08:58.977 --> 00:09:01.047
"Treatment technologies are needed..."

00:09:01.047 --> 00:09:04.990
Researchers pitched several new ideas 
throughout the years.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.740
The most recent would have looked like this:

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.520
waste would get flushed into a storage tank.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.590
The solids would get separated 
and sold as fertilizer,

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:17.652
and the liquids would go through a process
of removing nitrogen and phosphorus.

00:09:17.652 --> 00:09:20.920
The resulting less-harmful liquid 
would irrigate crops

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.690
or get reflushed through the system.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:27.620
This technology was able to get a state permit
for a pilot program

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:32.439
which showed that the new system substantially
eliminated water and air pollution.

00:09:32.439 --> 00:09:36.430
But it didn’t meet the economic feasibility
criterium.

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:38.810
It would’ve cost more than 
twice as much for farmers

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:41.010
than the lagoon and spray field system.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.000
So nothing changed.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.541
The answer from the industry said:

00:09:46.541 --> 00:09:51.498
“We can't afford to do it, 
it's not economically feasible.”

00:09:52.597 --> 00:09:57.580
Smithfield has decided to invest 
a half billion dollars in another technology.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:01.610
A “manure to energy project” mentioned
in this promotional video:

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.900
“We’re partnering with local 
contract farmers and leaders

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:08.360
in the bio energy field, for a 
groundbreaking project

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.200
underway in North Carolina.”

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.750
Which means they will be capturing 
the methane emitted from

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:18.440
at least 19 participating hog farms
in eastern North Carolina.

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:21.670
According to a map on 
the energy company’s web site

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:24.880
this pipeline will carry the methane 
to a processing facility

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.000
to be converted into natural gas

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.344
which will eventually end up in the state’s
existing natural gas pipeline.

00:10:31.344 --> 00:10:34.560
A version of this process is already happening
at this farm

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.070
in Lillington, North Carolina.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.510
Which was the last stop on our flight.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:41.258
My name is Tom Butler.

00:10:41.258 --> 00:10:45.620
I'm a contract hog farmer 
in Harney County, North Carolina.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:50.154
A contract farmer raises hogs for big, 
sometimes multinational companies

00:10:50.154 --> 00:10:52.829
that process the meat and sell it.

00:10:52.829 --> 00:10:56.923
BUTLER: They furnish all the pigs,
they furnish all the medication.

00:10:56.923 --> 00:11:01.500
BUTLER: They furnish the feed and then
the owner and operator of the farm

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:06.832
they raise the hogs, take care of them for
the 20 week period that we have them.

00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:11.201
Contract farmers build their facilities
to these corporation’s specifications

00:11:11.201 --> 00:11:15.220
which is why industrialized hog farms
look practically identical.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.443
And that includes waste management.

00:11:17.443 --> 00:11:22.651
BUTLER: We market about 20,000 adult hogs
here on the farm each year.

00:11:22.651 --> 00:11:26.300
And we produce about 10,000 gallons
of waste per day.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.025
And that's a lot of waste.

00:11:29.025 --> 00:11:32.340
Doing anything with waste above and beyond
what the contract pays for

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:37.160
is unaffordable for most farmers:
otherwise they can’t make a living.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:42.000
Which helps explain why farmers alone 
are unable to change this systemic problem.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.789
You cannot do it on your own.

00:11:43.789 --> 00:11:50.355
And the thing that we found out that the waste
management was not included in the contract.

00:11:50.355 --> 00:11:55.570
We didn't sit down when we signed the contract
and nobody laid out the paperwork

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:58.300
and says, "Look, now, 
you know,  in 25 years

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:02.360
you're going to have 
3 or 4 million gallons of sludge."

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.559
And you will have to clean it out, 
take it off the farm.

00:12:05.559 --> 00:12:08.070
And it's very, very expensive.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:10.305
The good thing it does for 
the environment is...

00:12:10.305 --> 00:12:12.170
In 2008, Tom received a grant

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:18.180
to put these green covers on his lagoons after
receiving complaints from his neighbors.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.460
We really didn't realize the impact 
we would have on our community.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:23.360
We have one neighbor, 
he called me one night

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.040
and he said that inside of his 
new living room

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.630
smelled like hog poop.

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:31.374
This helped mitigate some of the lagoon odor.

00:12:31.374 --> 00:12:35.840
Then, in 2012, through more loans and grants,
he took on a separate project

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.849
to install this digester which 
captures methane gas.

00:12:39.849 --> 00:12:44.228
That gas gets turned into energy 
for his farm with this generator.

00:12:44.228 --> 00:12:47.250
It’s a small-scale version of what 
Smithfield  is proposing

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.570
for the state’s electrical grid.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:54.210
Which makes Tom uniquely qualified to say
what this technology won’t do.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:58.490
The pipeline thing it doesn't improve 
the environmental impact.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:02.208
You still haven't solved any 
of your problems, except 

00:13:02.208 --> 00:13:04.562
you have sequestered 
some greenhouse gases

00:13:04.562 --> 00:13:08.240
which is a good thing, but 
it's not enough to justify

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:10.770
leaving the waste at the farm.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:15.215
Livestock and poultry manure contribute about
9% of US emissions of methane

00:13:15.215 --> 00:13:18.000
 a powerful greenhouse gas.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.900
Biogas operations have the potential
to reduce hog waste emissions by up to 85%.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.630
The natural gas it creates has 
the added climate benefit

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.080
of reducing our dependence on fossil fuels.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:35.170
And it will generate extra income for farmers
and the corporations they contract for.

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:36.890
When we reached Smithfield for comment

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:42.000
they said that “This is indisputably
a positive thing for the environment.”

00:13:44.325 --> 00:13:48.750
But these operations not only accept
the way large-scale hog facilities

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:52.370
currently operate:
they monetize it.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:56.540
Participating farms will still have to store
all the waste on their property

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:00.060
And spray their fields with it,
like Tom does.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:01.548
We still have the phosphorus issues.

00:14:01.548 --> 00:14:03.676
We still have nitrogen issues.

00:14:03.676 --> 00:14:09.810
We have some of the odor problems 
with spray fields and the runoff.

00:14:10.653 --> 00:14:15.366
That’s left communities to continue their fight
against powerful companies.

00:14:15.366 --> 00:14:18.420
Recent lawsuits against one of 
Smithfield’s subsidiaries

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:23.468
resulted in a nearly $100 million 
expected payout to 36 plaintiffs.

00:14:23.468 --> 00:14:27.370
And in 2021, community organizations 
filed yet another

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:31.029
civil rights complaint against 
the state of North Carolina

00:14:31.029 --> 00:14:34.160
alleging that the biogas pipeline 
would have a disproportionate impact

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.649
on communities of color,
due to its location.

00:14:37.649 --> 00:14:42.080
As planned, it would run very close
to Reverend Melvin’s church.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:49.344
I think this is a way for the industry
to say that they're focusing on clean energy.

00:14:49.344 --> 00:14:52.170
I'm really really nervous, 
what it's going to do

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:55.350
with communities that have already 
been impacted.

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:58.640
Communities and farmers will continue 
to pay the price

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:01.570
for the industrial hog industry’s 
waste problems,

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:05.520
until powerful corporations or 
state and federal governments

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.503
decide it’s worth the cost to fix.

00:15:08.503 --> 00:15:10.390
Those plans are out there.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:12.460
They're just too expensive.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:16.270
There's not enough money for me 
to take care of my waste.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:19.160
It's not about taking down 
the farming industry.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:24.589
We have family members, friends who work 
in the farming industry

00:15:24.589 --> 00:15:28.089
but it's a matter taking responsibility

00:15:28.089 --> 00:15:30.170
for the problem that they created.

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:34.860
They've made billions of dollars
off of these communities

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:39.230
and the resources have not been
put back into these communities.

00:15:41.376 --> 00:15:44.080
Thanks for watching this third episode 
of our series

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.710
with Vox's Future Perfect team.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:49.470
This is the last of our stories 
on the human impact

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:52.520
of industrialized meat production in the US.

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:57.130
This was a really ambitious series for us
in terms of reporting and filming.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:59.811
We cover consolidation in the beef industry
by way of

00:15:59.811 --> 00:16:02.300
a cattle auction in South Dakota.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.110
We covered worker safety issues 
in the poultry industry

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.490
through the work of a labor organizer in Arkansas.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.630
And for this story we went to North Carolina

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:13.929
to witness the environmental impact 
of hog farming.

00:16:13.929 --> 00:16:17.790
If you haven't already, check out episodes
1 and 2 in our channel.

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:21.420
And for more reporting on the meat industry
by the Future Perfect team

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:24.610
I would highly recommend checking out 
their podcast on the topic

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.360
which I linked below.

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.110
And stay tuned because we're going to produce

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:31.761
another set of stories with Future Perfect
on a totally different topic.

